## Speech Profile of Trenlor [bandit]
Voice and register vary by archetype — the cool opportunist sounds different from the cocky hot-head sounds different from the weary veteran. Pick one for the scene and let it color the lines.
What stays constant across them: rough working-class diction, crude humor about violence, slang for money and weapons. Profanity is normal but situational, not punctuation. Threats are matter-of-fact when they're real, theatrical when they're a bluff. When the fight goes bad and a no-win situation is clear, the speech shifts visibly — pleas, bargains, surrenders ("I yield, I yield" / "Take the gold, just walk" / "Mercy, traveler, I've got nothing left in this") rather than defiant last words. A bandit pleading for their life is in character; a bandit dying with a defiant one-liner is not.
